  "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant. \n\nMaking a substitution $x+y=u,$ $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ we get.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{y}^{1+y}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{y}^{1+y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{y+1}+\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y+1}]\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y+1}]\\,\\text{d}y & =[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y+1)]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{y}{y+1}){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{2}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
